[description]

[b]GitLab CI[/b] is configured in YAML checked into the repository. A [b]pipeline[/b] is created on push, MR, schedule, or web trigger; [b]stages[/b] order jobs; [b]needs:[/b] can build DAGs inside a stage for speed. Jobs run on [b]runners[/b] that poll for work—shared instance runners, group runners, or project-specific tokens on your hardware.

[b]Caches and artifacts:[/b] caches speed dependency installs; artifacts pass build outputs to downstream jobs or store test reports. Both consume disk—set expiry policies so shared runners do not fill.

[b]Environments and deployments:[/b] jobs can target named environments with URLs and optional manual approvals. That maps release process to Git branches or tags while surfacing history in the UI.

[b]Includes and components:[/b] large pipelines split across files with [code]include:[/code] and reusable [code]extends[/code] or component catalogs—treat them like libraries to avoid copy-paste YAML drift.

[b]Compared to GitHub Actions:[/b] different YAML shape and runner model, same idea: event-driven automation on the host that clones your repo and runs commands. Skills in writing idempotent scripts transfer.

[b]Takeaway:[/b] read pipelines as programs: stages are control flow, runners are machines, secrets are scoped credentials—never echo them to logs.
